# Swift/iOS security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to
iOS/Apple-platform-specific risks and the safe framework API to use
instead. Applies only to `*.swift` files.

## Secrets and credential storage

- Store credentials, tokens, and refresh tokens in the Keychain
  (`Security` framework, or a thin wrapper around it) — never in
  `UserDefaults`, which is unencrypted plist storage readable by
  anything with filesystem access to the app's container, and never in a
  plain file the app writes itself.
- Never hardcode an API key, client secret, or signing credential as a
  string literal in source. Load it from a build-time configuration
  (an `.xcconfig` value injected into `Info.plist`, a secrets manager
  fetched at runtime) that is excluded from source control, and treat a
  literal key found in a diff as a finding, not a style nit.
- Set an appropriate Keychain accessibility level
  (`kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly` or stricter) for
  anything sensitive — the default accessibility can survive a device
  backup/restore onto different hardware, which is usually not the
  intended threat model for a session token.

## Network security (ATS and transport)

- Leave App Transport Security (ATS) at its default (HTTPS-only,
  TLS 1.2+); do not add `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` or a blanket
  `NSExceptionDomains` bypass to `Info.plist` to work around a backend
  that is not yet on HTTPS — fix the backend, or scope an ATS exception
  to the exact domain and the exact minimum relaxation needed, with a
  comment saying why.
- Validate TLS the platform's normal way (`URLSession`'s default trust
  evaluation); do not implement a `URLSessionDelegate` that accepts any
  server certificate (`.performDefaultHandling` bypassed with
  unconditional trust) outside of a short-lived local/test
  configuration, and never ship that in production.

## Biometric authentication

- Treat a `LAContext` biometric (Face ID/Touch ID) result as a *local
  presence* gate, not proof of identity to a remote server on its own —
  a successful `evaluatePolicy` unlocks a locally-held secret (a
  Keychain item, a locally cached credential); it does not substitute
  for a real authentication token when talking to a backend.
- Check the specific `LAError` case on failure (`.userCancel`,
  `.biometryNotAvailable`, `.biometryLockout`, ...) and present a
  fallback (passcode, credential re-entry) rather than treating every
  failure identically or silently retrying.
- Do not cache "biometric succeeded" as a long-lived boolean flag read
  later by unrelated code paths — re-evaluate at the point access is
  actually needed, scoped to what that specific action requires.

## Input handling and injection-adjacent risks

- Parameterize any `NSPredicate`/Core Data query built from
  user-influenced input rather than interpolating the value directly
  into the predicate format string — the same injection-shaped risk as
  building SQL by string concatenation.
- Validate/sanitize any URL or deep link the app opens
  (`UIApplication.open`, a Universal Link handler) before acting on it —
  a deep link is untrusted input from outside the process, not a value
  to trust and route on directly.

## Logging

- Never log a token, password, or full PII payload at a level that
  reaches persistent device logs (`print`, unredacted `os_log`/`Logger`
  interpolation) — use `Logger`'s privacy-redaction (`\(value,
  privacy: .private)`) for anything sensitive, or omit it from the log
  entirely.
